{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>According to all that, etc.</b> In the Pesikta Rabbati (16) they said: When HaShem said to Moses, ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי״ etc., Moses was alarmed and said before Him: ״הֲלֹא הַשָּׁמַיִם וּשְׁמֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם לֹא יְכַלְכְּלוּהוּ״ etc., until the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: ״Not as you think, but twenty boards,״ etc. — end quote. Now one needs to know who stood in the counsel of YHWH, and Moses His servant, to know all the things that passed between them; and although all their words are words of received tradition, even so nothing is excluded from the written verses." "It appears possible to say, according to their words of blessed memory, that the difficulty which arose for them is that there is no clear sense to the verse either way: if it is drawn downward on this track — ״כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר etc. תַּעֲשׂוּ״ — He should not have said ״וְכֵן״ with the addition of a vav, but rather ״כֵּן תַּעֲשׂוּ״, which would be the grammatical continuation of what He said, ״כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר״ etc. And if the ״כְּכֹל״ refers back to His saying ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ״, He should not have interrupted by saying ״וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם״, but should have said ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר״ etc., and afterward say ״וְשָׁכַנְתִּי״ etc. And from the fact that He prefaced by saying ״וְשָׁכַנְתִּי״, HaShem revealed that He had completed the utterance of the general commandment, and there was no need to say in it ״כְּכֹל״ etc.; rather, He relied on what He would inform us of all the particulars of the work — the dimensions of the boards and all the vessels — as He said afterward. And from the fact that we see He afterward reconsidered to say ״כְּכֹל״ etc., the discerning person will understand that this was on account of matters that arose in the middle — without which, at the outset, what did He think, that He did not say ״כְּכֹל״, and in the end what did He think? And we shall be discerning to know what those matters that could arise in the middle are; from the answer you will know the question, and the answer is <b>כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי</b> etc. From this you have learned that He reconsidered further, and reason will justify and confirm this, and the further import of this is beyond measure. Your heart will understand, for there is indeed room for astonishment — namely their statement that he said, ״הֲלֹא שְׁמֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם״ etc., and because of this it was necessary to answer him, and He said to him <b>כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר</b> etc., and He showed him the form of the Mishkan in that vision. And the verse testifies to this from His saying <b>מַרְאֶה</b> — and He did not say אַרְאֶה for the future; and then he saw that it was twenty boards etc. As for the reason for the visual revelation, and why speech alone did not suffice, I will explain with the help of HaShem nearby." "<b>And this is the way</b> — an exalted one — to understand their words of blessed memory (Berakhot 55a), which they said regarding the verse (Exodus 38:22), ״וּבְצַלְאֵל etc. עָשָׂה אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה״ — even things that his teacher did not tell him, his own judgment agreed, etc. Moses commanded Bezalel to make the vessels first and afterward the Mishkan. Bezalel said to him: ״It is the way of the world to make a house first,״ etc. He [Moses] said to him: ״So I heard from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He — בְּצֵל אֵל הָיִיתָ, for surely so the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded me,״ etc. — end quote. And it is difficult: how could Moses forget the commandment of YHWH? Heaven forbid that such a thing be believed of him. And to understand the matter, one must note further that according to the order of the verses, I see that YHWH put the commandment of the vessels first — the Ark and the cover and its cherubim, then the Table, then the Menorah, then the Mishkan — and Moses is truth and his words are truth; why did he reverse his earlier words?" "Indeed, according to what we have explained — that when He said ״כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי מַרְאֶה אוֹתְךָ אֵת תַּבְנִית הַמִּשְׁכָּן״, then in that vision He showed him the Mishkan first, and when He specified for him the particulars of the work He put the vessels first — there are two ways in the words of YHWH: the first is that He put the Mishkan first, as is evident from verse 1, where it is written <b>אֵת תַּבְנִית הַמִּשְׁכָּן</b>; and what the Holy One, blessed be He, put first in the order of the work — the vessels — was not in order to begin with them, but rather He began from what He concluded, which is the form of the vessels. The second way is that the intention of the Holy One, blessed be He, was to put the vessels first, in the order that He stated in the particulars of the work. And the reason He put, in verse 1, <b>אֶת הַמִּשְׁכָּן</b> before the vessels is on account of the astonishment that Moses had — ״שָׁמַיִם וּשְׁמֵי שָׁמַיִם״ etc., as I have written above — which was only concerning the Mishkan; therefore the answer came by showing the Mishkan, that it is small. And incidentally the verse informed us that along with the form of the Mishkan He also showed him the form of the vessels that were in it. Or perhaps He also intended to say that there is no need for them to be enormously large, and when He came to arrange the order of the particulars of the work, He put the vessels first." "And Moses, peace be upon him, initially decided in interpreting the words of YHWH according to the second way, and when Bezalel reasoned before him he decided according to reason, following the first way. And therefore you will find that the midrash (Pesikta Rabbati) says that Moses said, ״Surely so the Holy One, blessed be He, said to me,״ and one needs to know why he said ״surely״? Rather, it is because there were before him the two ways mentioned, and at first he chose one of them, and now he retracted and said: surely the truth is according to the word of Bezalel. And since the Holy One, blessed be He, did not explicitly tell him which to put first, one cannot say of this, heaven forbid, that he forgot the words of YHWH." "And let it not be difficult in your eyes, according to our words, the language of the statement that Moses said: ״Thus I heard from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He.״ The intention is not to say that He explicitly stated the Tabernacle first, but the meaning is that according to the order of the matters, so it was heard." "And it remains to give a good reason why HaShem needed to show him the pattern of the Tabernacle. And perhaps, since the matters came only as a response to Moses' words when he said ״heavens and the heavens of heavens״ and so forth — therefore, had HaShem answered him ״twenty planks״ and so on, Moses would have heard that the measure came only to say that HaShem would accept and be pleased with so small a house without exertion, and the precise measure is not a commandment; and even if HaShem would say to him לֹא תוֹסִיף (you shall not add) צַו לָצָו (command upon command), despite all this there remains the possibility that it is for the reason of settling for little. Therefore HaShem showed him the pattern of the Tabernacle — a likeness of that which is above — and by this He justifies that this is His commandment, neither more nor less." "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, the verse alludes in saying in this order, that He prefaced <b>וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם</b> before saying <b>כְּכֹל</b>, to tell him a great matter: that the dimension of holiness and its gradations is not in the category of number, for it is high above high and higher ones. For you will find that they said, of blessed memory (Avot 3:7): one who sits and engages in Torah — the Shekhinah is with him, as it is said (Exodus 20:21): ״בְּכָל הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַזְכִּיר אֶת שְׁמִי״; and they spoke of two and three and five and ten, and each according to his gradation. And they further said (Genesis Rabbah 64) that the Shekhinah does not rest upon fewer than sixty myriads, for this reason the Torah was not given to the Patriarchs until the children numbered sixty myriads. From here you may infer and conclude that the gradations of holiness extend from one to sixty myriads — and this in the dimension of number — and in the dimension of quality there are how many gradations from one to one, without end. And since this is so, when HaShem said to Moses that He would dwell among them, Moses did not know the gradations of the dimension of the Indweller — what its measure is. Therefore HaShem was wise and spoke to him in these words: <b>וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי מַרְאֶה אוֹתְךָ</b> — He attached <b>כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר</b> and so forth to <b>וְשָׁכַנְתִּי</b>, to say: that very dimension that speaks with him, according to the measure of that chariot which He would show him, [that] shall dwell among them. And accordingly the word <b>כְּכֹל</b> and so forth is connected with <b>וְשָׁכַנְתִּי</b> and so forth." "<b>וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ.</b> Now, according to what I explained — that He showed him the pattern of the Tabernacle to say: thus you shall make, no less and no more — let this not be difficult for you: if so, there would have been no need to say ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״. For you will find that they said in Shevuot 17a, and this is their language: one who enters the Temple Courtyard and so forth — that they do not add to the city and the courtyards except with a king, a prophet, the Urim and Thummim, and the Sanhedrin. Whence these matters? Rav Shimi said: the verse says: ״כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי מַרְאֶה״ and so forth ״וְאֵת תַּבְנִית כָּל כֵּלָיו וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ — לְדוֹרוֹת (for the generations). See that ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ comes to command that they shall make in this order for the generations. And know that the exposition ״לְדוֹרוֹת״ — the Talmud derives it from the word ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״, not from the additional ו alone. For when we say that it derives only from the extra letter ו, there is no extra letter here, for if the verse had said ״כֵּן תַּעֲשׂוּ״, the intent of the declaration that the verse comes to inform us of — that the matters passed between Moses and HaShem — would not have been heard, and it would be difficult for me to hear the words of HaShem: why did He not say ״כְּכֹל״ and so forth before saying ״וְשָׁכַנְתִּי״? But surely the entire word ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ is superfluous. And so Rashi explained there in Shevuot, and this is his language: ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ — a superfluous verse for the midrash, לְדוֹרוֹת,״ end of quote. And the reason of Rashi, of blessed memory, is not for our reason, but rather because he explained ״כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי״ and so forth as connected above to ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ״ and so forth ״כְּכֹל״ and so forth — if so, the matter comes as a restraint, that one should not think to volunteer to make a greater one; if so, ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ — why do I need it? לְדוֹרוֹת (for the generations). And according to our approach, which followed the method of the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, that His saying <b>כְּכֹל</b> and so forth comes to respond to Moses with a restraint of the measure, from what He showed him — like its counterpart in heaven — from this it will be felt that they should not change it." "<b>And I saw</b> to the Rav Ibn Ezra that he explained ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ as referring to the vessels, since at the beginning only ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ״ was mentioned. And the Rav did not give attention to state a reason why it was necessary to say ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ about the vessels, nor was it sufficient with what is commanded in the immediate continuation regarding the specification of all the vessels. Granted, what He said — ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ״ — is the general statement of the commandment and its reason: that a sanctuary be made for HaShem to dwell within it, and afterward He stated the manner of construction; but this reason does not hold in the statement ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ regarding the making of the vessels — unless you say as the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, who derived it לְדוֹרוֹת (for the generations). And I do not know why he hides his eyes from the Or HaChayim that our Rabbis of blessed memory illuminated." "It also seems wondrous to me — the words of Ramban, z\"l, who took issue with the words of Rashi, who said: ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ — for all generations. And he said: ״And I do not know whether this is true, that Solomon would be obligated to make the vessels of the Eternal House according to this template — and the bronze altar he made twenty cubits long and twenty cubits wide״ (II Chronicles 4:1), end quote. From his words it appears that the sugya of tractate Shevuot was hidden from his memory, for the words of Rashi are the words of the Gemara, and what room is there to disagree with the Talmud? And it would seem reasonable to say that Ramban holds that what the Talmud said — ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for all generations — the intent is not regarding the measurements of the vessels but regarding the matters stated there that require a king and a prophet, etc., and seventy-one of the Sanhedrin, and his words do not appear reasonable, for who says so [literally: who obligates]. And further, according to his words, what is the Gemara there asking from the anointing of vessels, which sanctifies vessels that Moses made but not other vessels that someone other than Moses made — and the Talmud needed to bring them a proof from the verse, when it could have said that the verse did not say ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for all generations regarding all the details, but only regarding the matters enumerated there in the Mishnah?" "<b>Rather</b>, one must resolve the difficulty of Ramban from the bronze altar that Solomon made. And I saw the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, who wished to resolve it and said that the intent of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ is not regarding the measurement literally, but regarding the model of the proportion of the structure — the width relative to the length, and the height relative to its length and width — but as for the overall size, there is no exacting requirement. And therefore, just as the altar that Moses made was 5 by 5, likewise for all generations they made it square, twenty by twenty. And the Rav drew support from the words of Tosafot in the chapter HaZorek (Shabbat 98b), who asked regarding what is heard from the words of the Talmud — that the width of the Mishkan was ten cubits — from where do they know this, and perhaps its width was eleven cubits, etc. And Rabbi Yehudah answered: we learn it from the Eternal House, which was sixty cubits long and twenty wide — so too in the Mishkan, its width was a third of its length, and it is written ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״, end quote. And the Rav raised an objection to this: if so, why did he not make the height of the altar according to the proportion of the altar of Moses, which was three in height for five of length and width, and according to this ratio the altar of Solomon, which was twenty by twenty, should have been twelve high. And he answered that thus the halakhah came — that ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ was said only regarding length and width alone, and not regarding the height, end quote." "<b>And according to</b> my opinion, the words of the Rav do not appear reasonable, for what he wrote — that ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for all generations comes only upon the proportion of the structure and not upon its measurement — this cannot be accepted from him without proof from the Talmud. And if because of the difficulty, it is more fitting to leave the matter as requiring investigation than to innovate from our own minds things that have nothing on which to rely. And what he proved from the answer of Rabbi Yehudah — I take issue with that as well, and furthermore it is no proof, for Rabbi Yehudah did not base his answer on the derashah of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״. And what the Rav wrote in transcribing the words of Rabbi Yehudah — ״and it is written וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ — these are not the words of Rabbi Yehudah but rather the Rav said so to justify his own words. And the meaning of Rabbi Yehudah's words is that he is stating a revealed matter, for from the fact that we see that in the Eternal House it was twenty by sixty, and here in the Mishkan there is a doubt whether it was twenty long and ten wide or eleven, the doubt will be resolved from the Eternal House. Or perhaps they had some proof to learn this arrangement from the Eternal House and not from the derashah of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״. And this is your sign: that Rabbi Yehudah did not mention the derashah of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ to strengthen his answer. And furthermore, what the Rav added to raise a difficulty from the height and said that the halakhah came from Mount Sinai that ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ was said only upon the proportion of the square and not upon the proportion of the height to the measurement of the square." "<b>Know</b> that our Sages, of blessed memory (Zevachim 59b), disagreed — Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Yosi. Rabbi Yosi holds that all the bronze altars, whether what Moses made, or what Solomon made, or what the exiles made, and what is destined to be made — all are ten cubits in height, each one of them, and the three cubits written in the Torah — that is the place of the ma'arakhah alone, end quote. And this opinion is halakhah (Rambam, Hilkhot Beit HaBechirah, ch. 2), and the sugya of the chapter Shtei HaLechem (Menachot 97a) affirms according to this opinion. And according to this opinion, the difficulty of the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, is no difficulty at all, and Rabbi Yehudah disagrees and holds: the matter is as written — three cubits its height. Now, the difficulty of the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, is only valid according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, and it would have been proper for him not to speak his words in a manner of simplicity, as though they are agreed-upon matters, which is not so. And what the Rav, of blessed memory, answered — that thus the halakhah came, etc. — from the sugya of Shevuot this is not implied, from what the Talmud said: ״the amah-batlin that they do not add onto the courtyards, etc. — from where are these things?״ and Rav Shimi answered: the verse says ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for all generations, end quote. Behold, from the fact that the Talmud asks ״from where are these things״ and Rav Shimi needed to answer — hear from this that the questioner did not know the derashah of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for all generations. And from the conclusion of the matter you know that he also did not know the halakhah that the Rav stated — which applies to the height, which is not for all generations — for if he had known the halakhah, he would necessarily have known the derashah of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for all generations, from whose plain meaning the halakhah is derived. And since this is so, why did the questioner not himself raise the difficulty from the height of the altar, that Solomon did not make it like that of Moses — which is precisely the difficulty of the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, himself — for it has no answer except through the halakhah that applies to this, and this halakhah the questioner did not know, as mentioned. And there is further proof from the depth of the sugya that there was no halakhah in this matter, and the words of the Rav are not precise." "<b>And I</b> say that there is no difficulty from Ramban regarding the altar that was twenty by twenty, and no difficulty from Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi regarding the height — it is no difficulty at all. For behold, before you is a baraita arranged in the chapter Kodshei Kodashim (Zevachim 62a), and this is its language: ״The Sages taught: horn, ramp, base, and square shape are indispensable; the measure of its length and the measure of its width and the measure of its height are not indispensable.״ And the Talmud says: from where are these things? The verse says ״הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ — to require, end quote. Behold, that the altar itself that Moses made could have been made twenty by twenty, and the height as well could have been made more than three cubits. And if so, what room is there for the difficulty of the Rav from the altar that Solomon made, for the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed that height and width and length are not indispensable? And also the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, z\"l — the words of the baraita were hidden from his eyes, and he thought that Moses could not have made the altar larger than this measurement, and that the height too must be five [in length and width] by three in height and no more, and he needed to give the two forced answers. And behold, I have planted for you a tree planted by the waters, and you may rely on it without faltering: for the saying of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ speaks of the things that are indispensable in the altar, which are: horn, ramp, base, and square shape, as taught in the baraita, and also that it not be less than five cubits wide and five cubits long, as they said there in the chapter Kodshei Kodashim, provided only that one not diminish it from the altar that Moses made." "<b>Rather,</b> what remains for us to know is whether the other details of the Tabernacle were indispensable, while in the Eternal Temple they were not indispensable. And on the contrary, Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of Hilkhot Beit HaBechirah that they build only with stones or with bricks. Now, I have examined all that Rambam wrote in the laws of the House of our Elohim, and I see that regarding three vessels he wrote that they must be exactly like the vessels that Moses made in the wilderness — and they are: the Table, the Menorah, and the golden Altar — and so it is explicit in the chapter on the two loaves (Menahot 99b). But the other vessels and the form of the House — one does not take a measure for the generations from the work of Moses in the wilderness. According to this, it would have appeared reasonable to say that the commandment of ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for the generations applies only to the vessels. But this is not so, for in the Gemara they expounded it regarding the consecration of the Courtyard, and this tells us that the commandment for the generations applied also to the House. Moreover, even regarding the vessels, I see that Solomon made a second pair of cherubim, very different from the cherubim that Moses made (I Kings 6), and how many changes there were in the other vessels." "<b>Now,</b> with a little reflection on the matter there is no difficulty whatsoever, for one must know that the entire structure of the House and its vessels Solomon did not make according to what appeared fitting in the eye of his intellect to glorify, but according to the word of a prophet — and even the prophet declared that what he conveyed were not the words of his own prophecy but the words of his received tradition, as it is written in Chronicles (I Chronicles 28:11): ״וַיִּתֵּן דָּוִד לִשְׁלֹמֹה בְּנוֹ תַּבְנִית הָאוּלָם״ and so forth, ״וְתַבְנִית כָּל אֲשֶׁר הָיָה בָרוּחַ עִמּוֹ״ and so forth, ״הַכֹּל בִּכְתָב מִיַּד ה׳ עָלַי הִשְׂכִּיל כָּל מְלֶאכֶת הַתַּבְנִית״ and so forth. And it is further written (Rashi on I Chronicles 28:12): ״אֲשֶׁר הָיָה בָרוּחַ עִמּוֹ כַּאֲשֶׁר הֶרְאָהוּ שְׁמוּאֵל הָרוֹאֶה״, and they said, of blessed memory, in the Aggadah of Samuel (Midrash Samuel, chapter 15): Rabbi Yirmeyah in the name of Rabbi Shimon, and so forth — the Holy One, blessed be He, delivered the Temple to Moses in its standing form, and Moses delivered it to Joshua in its standing form, and so forth; Joshua delivered it to the elders in its standing form, as it is written ״וַיֶּאֱסֹף יְהוֹשֻׁעַ״ and so forth, ״וַיִּתְיַצְּבוּ״ and so forth; the elders delivered it to the prophets in its standing form; the prophets delivered it to David in its standing form, and so forth; David to Solomon, and so forth. And it says ״הַכֹּל בִּכְתָב״ and so forth — this teaches that it was given to be expounded; ״מִיַּד ה׳״ — this teaches that it was given by tradition; ״עָלַי הִשְׂכִּיל״ — this teaches that it was given through the holy spirit; end quote. You now see to know that everything Solomon did in the House was a halakhah to Moses from Sinai, and all is written in the book, revealed to those who seek Torah through the holy spirit." "And so you will find that Rashi wrote in his commentary on Chronicles (I Chronicles 28:19), and this is his language: ״And all of it Samuel expounded from the Torah through the holy spirit, as we learned: Mount of the House was five hundred cubits by five hundred cubits; he expounded — the length of the court one hundred in cubits, and the width fifty by fifty, and so forth — they come to five hundred by five hundred,״ end quote. And from this it follows that the entire design is alluded to in the Torah for those who understand through the holy spirit. Accordingly, what the verse says — ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״ for the generations — applies to those matters regarding which no reversal appears in the Torah, as is intellectually grasped by those who understand knowledge through the holy spirit. In these they shall proceed according to the design described in the wilderness Tabernacle; but as for matters stated in the Torah concerning the law of the Eternal Temple, there will be no contradiction from the verse ״וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ״, since there is room to maintain that it applies to the other details upon which no halakhah was handed down. And understand this." "<b>And by this approach</b> the snare of Ramban's difficulty regarding Rashi is also loosened, and the pain of Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrachi over the matter of the height of the altar is also healed — even if one were to say that the height of the wilderness altar was indispensable." "<b>And since</b> our Sages of blessed memory said that the building of the Eternal Temple was given to be expounded from the Torah, I said I would take a portion in this exposition in two or three details. And this is the first: At the beginning of the word of HaShem He said <b>וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ</b>, and He concluded by saying <b>תַּבְנִית הַמִּשְׁכָּן</b> — whereas He should have said ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִשְׁכָּן״. From this you learn that He commanded regarding two sanctuaries, as we alluded at the beginning of the discussion. And He alluded to its design by saying ״fifty by fifty,״ as the prophet Samuel, peace be upon him, explained; and if this is not relevant to the wilderness Tabernacle, assign it to the meaning of the Eternal Temple alluded to in His saying <b>וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ.</b> From this it follows that the statement <b>וְכֵן תַּעֲשׂוּ</b> for the generations does not apply to this particular detail of the measure of the Temple's design — and it still remains in its place for the other matters that they, of blessed memory, expounded in Shevuot (15a) regarding a prophet and a Sanhedrin, and so forth." "<b>Further</b>, we find (I Kings 6:23) that Solomon made two cherubim additional to those that Moses made, so there were four cherubim which HaShem had not commanded; and they are also changed by a great difference from the cherubim of Moses." "<b>Now it appears</b> that the verse alluded to them in what it said (Exodus 25:18): ״וְעָשִׂיתָ שְׁנַיִם כְּרֻבִים וְגוֹ׳ מִשְּׁנֵי קְצוֹת הַכַּפֹּרֶת, וַעֲשֵׂה כְּרוּב אֶחָד וְגוֹ׳ וּכְרוּב אֶחָד״ and so forth. And it is difficult: why was it necessary to say the act of making twice — ״וְעָשִׂיתָ״ ״וַעֲשֵׂה״? It is further difficult, for the entire verse is superfluous, since from His saying ״מִשְּׁנֵי קְצוֹת הַכַּפֹּרֶת״ it is already understood that one cherub is from one end and so forth. It is further difficult: why did He additionally say ״עַל שְׁנֵי קְצוֹתָיו״?" "Now, HaShem alluded to the making of the two cherubim that would be made in the eternal house, besides the cherubim that Moses would make. Corresponding to what would be made for the time being, He said <b>וְעָשִׂיתָ</b> in the second person, for he would make them in the Tabernacle; and corresponding to the cherubim of the eternal house, which He alluded to at the beginning of His words, He said <b>וַעֲשֵׂה</b> — He did not address him in the second person but as one commanding about another making at another time: that besides the two cherubim mentioned, which are from the ends of the cover, yet another making would be done — one cherub from the end from this — and these stand independently, as Solomon indeed made them (I Kings 6:23–32), for their feet touched the ground and not the edge of the cover. And the meaning of <b>מִקָּצָה</b> is: at the place where its edge terminates; or perhaps the word <b>מִזֶּה</b> is intended to mean בְּתֵבַת מִזֶּה, to say: besides this, and it refers back to the cherub He had mentioned. And afterward the verse returned to stipulate and said: corresponding to the cherubim that Moses would make, He said <b>מִן הַכַּפֹּרֶת</b>, meaning from the body of the cover itself — <b>תַּעֲשׂוּ אֶת הַכְּרוּבִים</b> that I mentioned at the beginning of My words, two cherubim. And in order that you not err and think it refers back to the second cherubim, therefore He mentioned a second time <b>תַּעֲשׂוּ</b>, which He should not have needed to say since He had already said ״וַעֲשֵׂה״ — but rather to say that He is not speaking about the making of the Temple cherubim but about the making of Moses' cherubim He is speaking; therefore He said in the second person <b>תַּעֲשׂוּ.</b> Further, HaShem alerted to make you understand thus, in that He said <b>אֶת הַכְּרוּבִים</b>, which He should not have needed to mention since He is occupied with them, and He should have said only תַּעֲשֵׂם or תַּעֲשֶׂה אוֹתָם; and by His saying <b>תַּעֲשׂוּ אֶת הַכְּרוּבִים</b> He revealed that He is not speaking about what is before Him but about what is before what is before Him, as we have explained. And His saying <b>עַל שְׁנֵי קְצוֹתָיו</b> means: they must stand on the edge of the cover — lest you say, it is true that they should be from the body of the cover, but it is possible that he would extend their legs and stand them on the ground and not on the cover of the ark; therefore He said <b>עַל שְׁנֵי קְצוֹתָיו</b>, meaning that their placement should be literally on the edges of the cover — which He did not so stipulate regarding the two of the eternal house." "<b>And although</b> you will find that in the making that Bezalel did, the verse said in the order of everything stated, so that it appears that everything stated in the verse exists in the making of the wilderness Tabernacle — and our Sages of blessed memory explained the reason for the doubling, that you should not say to make two for every end and so forth; see there in the words of Rashi (Exodus 25:19) — nevertheless, if it were not for the reason that He wished to allude to the second cherubim that would be made in the future, He should not have first written indefinitely ״וְעָשִׂיתָ שְׁנַיִם כְּרוּבִים״ and then come back to remove the error, lest you think two from every side, and say ״כְּרוּב אֶחָד״ and so forth; and from the start He should have written ״וְעָשִׂיתָ כְּרוּב אֶחָד מִקְּצֵה הַכַּפֹּרֶת וּכְרוּב אֶחָד וְגוֹ׳ תַּעֲשֶׂה אוֹתָם״. But surely He intended to allude as I have explained, and the plain sense of the verses remains in its place — for do you not find what Shmuel explained in the verse (Exodus 27:18) ״חֲמִשִּׁים בַּחֲמִשִּׁים״ — this is not the plain sense of the verse but a derashah, and the plain sense was not uprooted. So too according to our way, although the plain sense exists, there is also the derashah by force of the difficulty we raised just above. And further you will find that here, in the command of HaShem, He was precise to say the word <b>וַעֲשֵׂה</b> to intimate what we explained in it — whereas in the making of Bezalel, He did not say ״וַיַּעַשׂ״; this suffices as a note." "<b>And the golden altar</b> — we find (I Kings 7:48) that Solomon made another and stored away that of Moses, as is explained in the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Menachot 99a): regarding the table and the menorah that Moses made, there is a dispute whether they would arrange and kindle only in it, or also in the other tables and menorot that Solomon made they would sometimes arrange and kindle in this one and sometimes in that one. But the golden altar is a matter of agreement that it was stored away and did not serve except with Solomon's. And one needs to know: why did he store away that of Moses?" "<b>And it will be seen</b> that the Torah alluded to this matter by uprooting the order of the commandment of the golden altar from the place where it was fitting to command concerning it. For you will find that it began first with the Ark, which is the innermost of the inner, and after that the cover, and after that the Table, and after that the Menorah, and after them the Mishkan — its coverings, its planks, its pillars, its sockets — and after it the outer altar, and after it the courtyard of the Mishkan, and after it the oil and the garments of service for Aaron and the inauguration offerings and the daily offerings, and after all this He commanded concerning the golden altar. And in this there is a great impression [of intent] in so great a departure, and He alluded by this that its law is altered from [that of] all the Mishkan and its vessels. For the Mishkan and its vessels that are fit for the eternal house will serve in the eternal house — such as the Table and the Menorah, as we have written above, that they served in the eternal house. And whatever is not fit for the eternal house — such as the Mishkan and vessels that did not serve in the Temple, and likewise the outer altar, where the requirement was only for the square shape, and in the Temple they needed to make it larger on account of the need — [is different]. But the golden altar, even though its service would not change — which is the burning of half a measure in the morning and half a measure in the evening — nevertheless it would not serve in the eternal house, and He would make another and store the first one away hidden away, which He did not do so with the Table and the Menorah that Moses made — for although he made ten, that of Moses outweighed them all. And the matter of the allusions to the work of the eternal house is nothing but a mere allusion."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :register {:flags {10 ["the Eternal"], 11 ["the Eternal"], 12 ["the Eternal"], 15 ["the Eternal"], 17 ["the Eternal"], 19 ["the Eternal"]}}, :notes {14 "The phrase ״עֵץ שָׁתוּל עַל מַיִם״ (a tree planted by the waters) alludes to Jeremiah 17:8 and is rendered literally as it functions as the Or HaChaim's poetic signature for a secure resolution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:57.164943229Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}